# Remediation Master Plan: Production-Readiness Overhaul

## Executive Summary & Architectural Rationale

Following our comprehensive, four-part audit across the entire codebase (**60 total issues found**), this Master Plan establishes a deterministic, dependency-ordered roadmap for complete remediation.

### The Dependency Execution Order

Fixing multi-tier distributed systems requires a strict **bottom-up, reverse-dependency execution sequence**:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Phase 1: packages/shared                                    │
│  - TypeScript types, Enums, Zod schemas, dual ESM/CJS build  │
└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘
                               │
                ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐
                ▼                             ▼
┌──────────────────────────────┐ ┌──────────────────────────────┐
│  Phase 2: apps/worker        │ │  Phase 3: apps/server        │
│  - Sandbox safety & limits   │ │  - Routing fixes & auth      │
│  - Idempotency & DLQ         │ │  - Rate limits & aggregations│
│  - Container leak prevention │ │  - Live sample run endpoint  │
└──────────────────────────────┘ └──────────────┬───────────────┘
                                                │
                                                ▼
                                 ┌──────────────────────────────┐
                                 │  Phase 4: apps/client        │
                                 │  - Real sample runner UI     │
                                 │  - Error boundaries & polling│
                                 │  - Design system CSS & a11y  │
                                 └──────────────┬───────────────┘
                                                │
                                                ▼
                                 ┌──────────────────────────────┐
                                 │  Phase 5: Full Verification  │
                                 │  - Type checks across repo   │
                                 │  - Unit & integration tests  │
                                 │  - Production bundle builds  │
                                 └──────────────────────────────┘
```

#### Why This Order Prevents "Bug-Fix-Bug Loops":
1. **`packages/shared` First**: Types (`ISolution`, `ISubmissionResponse`, `ProblemDifficulty`), constants (`Verdicts`, `PROBLEM_DIFFICULTIES`), and Zod schemas form the contract layer. If we fix consuming code before stabilizing the contract, we would build against broken shapes.
2. **`apps/worker` Second**: Establishes correct sandbox evaluation, metric parsing, fail-safes, and container lifecycle management.
3. **`apps/server` Third**: Resolves route shadowing, implements the real `POST /api/submissions/run` endpoint (backed by worker capabilities), adds BOLA/IDOR protection, and enforces atomic counters and strict rate limits.
4. **`apps/client` Fourth**: Fixes UI bugs, replaces mock sample runners with real API connections, adds global error boundaries, removes Tailwind classes in favor of the custom CSS system, and enforces type safety against the now-stable backend contract.
5. **Phase 5 Full Verification**: End-to-end integration and build validation across all workspaces.

